{
  "title": "Draft, Sketch, and Prove: Guiding Formal Theorem Provers with Informal Proofs",
  "authors": [
    "Albert Q. Jiang",
    "Sean Welleck",
    "Jin Peng Zhou",
    "Wenda Li",
    "Jiacheng Liu",
    "Mateja Jamnik",
    "Timothe\u0301e Lacroix",
    "Yuhuai Wu",
    "Guillaume Lample"
  ],
  "year": 2023,
  "arxivId": "2210.12283",
  "abstract": "The formalization of existing mathematical proofs is a notoriously difficult process. This work introduces Draft, Sketch, and Prove (DSP), a method that maps informal proofs to formal proof sketches, and uses the sketches to guide an automated prover by directing its search to easier sub-problems. The DSP method achieves state-of-the-art performance on the miniF2F dataset with the Isabelle theorem prover.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The Draft, Sketch, and Prove (DSP) method uses informal proofs to guide automated formal theorem provers. It works by <strong>drafting informal proofs</strong>, <strong>mapping them to formal proof sketches</strong>, and <strong>proving the remaining conjectures</strong> using an automated prover. This approach <strong>leverages the abundance of informal mathematical data</strong> and <strong>combines the strengths of informal and formal mathematics</strong>.",
